posts.body:
JohnDoeGames said:
Wow. I kinda thought the static imaged was one of the limitations of RPG maker... This is helping me move forward so much. $gameScreen._pictures(1)._angle is exactly what I was looking for.
I'm not so sure about the loop though, since I want to use it in a plugin. I mean, I could use it in a loop still... In a
for (n = pic._angle ; n < 30 ; n++)
or something.
You guys have given me a lot to play with. Thank you very much.
Click to expand...
When using a plugin, your loop will run the code immediately and return to you the final result instantly. You will need to tie it into the Game_Picture.prototype.update() method which updates the code every frame. This allows you to process it frame by frame rather than all of it together within one frame.
This is why event commands are popular because you can event the code, use wait frames, and update it within the Game_Interpreter (the event commands and script calls that reference "this").
An example to look at is the updateRotation() method (and the other update methods like updateMove() or updateTone()) that can help you establish a baseline for your plugin/script call:
JavaScript:
Game_Picture.prototype.updateRotation = function() {
    if (this._rotationSpeed !== 0) {
        this._angle += this._rotationSpeed / 2;
    }
};
This code is updated every frame in the Game_Picture.prototype.update() method (which is updated by Game_Screen, etc.):
JavaScript:
Game_Picture.prototype.update = function() {
    this.updateMove();
    this.updateTone();
    this.updateRotation();
};
Alias the .update() method and it should process it frame by frame along with everything else.
Edit:
If you are eventing, it would look like this:
- Show Picture 1
- Loop 1:
- Wait 1 frame, increase the angle of picture 1 by 1 degree
- Check for when the angle of picture 1 is 30 degrees and if it is, break the loop
- Loop 2:
- Wait 1 frame, decrease the angle of picture by 1 degree
- Check for when the angle of picture 1 is 0 degrees and if it is, break the loop
